data.content:
**Yes, C# fully supports `stackalloc` into a `Span<nint>` and `Span<nuint>`.** The syntax is straightforward:

```csharp
Span<nint>  nints  = stackalloc nint[4];
Span<nuint> nuints = stackalloc nuint[3];
```

On this 64-bit system, `nint`/`nuint` are 8 bytes each. The only caveat encountered is that `sizeof(nint)` requires an `unsafe` context (since the size isn't fixed at compile time), but you can use `nint.Size` instead.
